{"metadata": {"file_suffix": "pdf", "producer": {"name": "mineru", "version": "4.0.10"}, "document": {"created_at": "2026-10-24T03:54:53Z", "modified_at": "2026-10-24T03:54:53Z", "creator_application": "TeX", "producer_application": "pdfTeX-1.40.29", "page_count": 3, "page_count_kind": "physical"}}, "extensions": {"mineru": {"tier": "standard", "parse_mode": "txt"}, "docvortex_layout": {"version": 1, "pages": [{"page_idx": 0, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 1, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}, {"page_idx": 2, "width_pt": 612.0, "height_pt": 792.0}]}}, "pages": [{"page_idx": 0, "blocks": [{"type": "page_number", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.115, 0.045, 0.129, 0.059], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "1"}]}, {"type": "header", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.768, 0.044, 0.885, 0.061], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Anant Gupta"}]}, {"type": "doc_title", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.356, 0.139, 0.644, 0.168], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Writing #2: "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { D } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " Lite"}], "level": 1}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.43, 0.184, 0.572, 0.204], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Anant Gupta"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.441, 0.207, 0.559, 0.225], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "CSCI 4511W"}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.114, 0.275, 0.268, 0.295], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "1. Summary"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.111, 0.314, 0.887, 0.485], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "This report covers "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "``D ^{*}"}, {"type": "text", "content": " Lite” by Koenig and Likhachev [3]."}, {"type": "text", "content": "1", "styles": ["superscript"]}, {"type": "text", "content": " A robot must reach a goal on a grid map, not knowing every wall in advance. It assumes that every unseen cell is free and plans a shortest path. When it observes a blocked cell, it replans. Rerunning a conventional graph search each time can take minutes on a large map, yet one new obstacle changes the goal distance of only a few cells [3]. "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { D } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " Lite recomputes only the changed cells that matter for the path."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.112, 0.495, 0.887, 0.697], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The method builds on Lifelong Planning "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { A ^ { * } ( L P A ^ { * } ) }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " [3], an incremental version of the "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " algorithm of Hart, Nilsson, and Raphael [2]. Every cell stores "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "g ,"}, {"type": "text", "content": " its current distance estimate, and rhs, a one-step lookahead based on its neighbors. If both match, the cell is consistent. Only inconsistent cells are kept in a priority queue, sorted by a key whose first part corresponds to the "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " value "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "f = g + h"}, {"type": "text", "content": " . When an edge cost changes, the affected cells are updated and the queue is processed until the robot’s cell is consistent again. The very first search expands the same cells that "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " would [3]."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 8, "bbox": [0.111, 0.706, 0.886, 0.878], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Two changes transform "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { L P A ^ { * } }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " into "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { D } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " Lite. First, the search proceeds backward from the goal, so the stored values are distances to the goal and stay valid while the robot moves. Second, movement makes the heuristic stored in each queued key out of date. Rather than reordering the queue, "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { D } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " Lite adds a running offset "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "k _ { m }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " to each key and rechecks a cell when it is dequeued. The result behaves like Stentz’s Focussed "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { D } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " [5], but it is shorter and easier to analyze [3]."}]}, {"type": "page_footnote", "index": 9, "bbox": [0.111, 0.885, 0.885, 0.917], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "1", "styles": ["superscript"]}, {"type": "text", "content": "Generative AI use: I used Claude (Anthropic) to research the four paper options, extract and read the paper’s text, and draft this report. I checked each claim against the paper and edited the final text myself."}]}, {"type": "page_number", "index": 10, "bbox": [0.494, 0.938, 0.505, 0.951], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "1"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 1, "blocks": [{"type": "header", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.115, 0.045, 0.129, 0.06], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "2"}]}, {"type": "header", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.768, 0.044, 0.885, 0.061], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Anant Gupta"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.111, 0.096, 0.887, 0.266], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The authors state that a search terminates with a correct path and expands each cell at most twice, with proofs in a separate technical report. In a simulation over eight-connected random grids, "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { D } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " Lite outperformed Focussed "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { D } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " on vertex expansions, heap percolates, and vertex accesses. It also outperformed its variants that omit the incremental search (which is repeated "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { A } ^ { * } )"}, {"type": "text", "content": " or the heuristic, on almost every comparison, by more than a factor of seven in the vertex expansions, at best [3]."}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.113, 0.305, 0.266, 0.325], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "2. Response"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "text", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.112, 0.344, 0.887, 0.545], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The paper targets replanning on a graph where edge costs change over time, with navigation in unknown terrain as the main example. It handles any pattern of cost changes, but reuse should pay off most when few costs change at once and a good heuristic is available. The heuristic must never overestimate and must satisfy a triangle inequality, which the authors note holds when it comes from relaxing the problem [3]. The model is narrow: the robot observes which of its eight neighbors are blocked, and the paper does not address noisy sensors or actions that can fail."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.112, 0.555, 0.887, 0.816], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The theorems support correctness, but the proofs are in a technical report, so I was unable to check them from the paper alone. The experiments are simulation-only, as "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { D } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " had already been demonstrated on real robots. The authors report vertex expansions, heap percolates, and vertex accesses rather than run time. Counts do not depend on the machine, and 50 random terrains per size with 95 percent confidence intervals support the claim that "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { D } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " Lite is at least as efficient as "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { D } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " [3]. I would still want the wall-clock time, as a count does not say much about a single operation’s cost. One result is easy to miss: on the adaptiveresolution grids, "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { D } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " Lite and its variant without the heuristic ran about equally well, so the heuristic is not always beneficial."}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.111, 0.827, 0.886, 0.907], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "The paper is easy to follow for a reader familiar with "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " . The pseudocode has numbered lines, and the analysis repeatedly compares "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { L P A ^ { * } }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " to "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " , which anchors it in the textbook treatment of "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { ~ A ^ { * } ~ } [ 4 ]"}, {"type": "text", "content": " . The "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "k _ { m }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " argument is the hardest part to follow. Implementing the"}]}, {"type": "page_number", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.493, 0.938, 0.506, 0.951], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "2"}]}]}, {"page_idx": 2, "blocks": [{"type": "page_number", "index": 0, "bbox": [0.115, 0.045, 0.129, 0.059], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "2"}]}, {"type": "header", "index": 1, "bbox": [0.768, 0.044, 0.885, 0.061], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "Anant Gupta"}]}, {"type": "text", "index": 2, "bbox": [0.111, 0.096, 0.885, 0.175], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "algorithm from the pseudocode looks feasible, but reproducing the exact numbers does not, since the paper does not describe how the random terrains were generated or where the start and goal cells were placed."}], "continues_prev": true}, {"type": "text", "index": 3, "bbox": [0.111, 0.186, 0.886, 0.387], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "What I found most interesting is how little is added to "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " . The first search is simply "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " , and the incremental behavior comes from keeping g between searches, adding rhs, and carrying one number, "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "k _ { m }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " . In the textbook, "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { A } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " runs once on a fixed graph [4]; here, the question is what to keep as the graph changes. The paper also looks useful beyond theoretical interest: it mentions that "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { D } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " has been used on real robots, including outdoor vehicles [3], and AAAI gave it a Classic Paper Honorable Mention in 2019 [1]. A follow-up I would test is how obstacle density affects the run-time gap between "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { D } ^ { * }"}, {"type": "text", "content": " Lite and repeated "}, {"type": "equation_inline", "content": "\\mathrm { A } ^ { * }"}]}, {"type": "paragraph_title", "index": 4, "bbox": [0.114, 0.44, 0.277, 0.46], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "3. References"}], "level": 2}, {"type": "ref_text", "index": 5, "bbox": [0.116, 0.468, 0.882, 0.522], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "[1] Association for the Advancement of Artificial Intelligence. AAAI classic paper award. https://aaai.org/about-aaai/aaai-awards/aaai-classic-paper-award/. Accessed October 23, 2026."}]}, {"type": "ref_text", "index": 6, "bbox": [0.116, 0.535, 0.885, 0.59], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "[2] Hart, P. E., Nilsson, N. J., and Raphael, B. A formal basis for the heuristic determination of minimum cost paths. IEEE Transactions on Systems Science and Cybernetics 4, 2 (1968), 100–107."}]}, {"type": "ref_text", "index": 7, "bbox": [0.117, 0.602, 0.885, 0.657], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "[3] Koenig, S., and Likhachev, M. D* Lite. In Proceedings of the Eighteenth National Conference on Artificial Intelligence (AAAI-02) (Edmonton, Alberta, Canada, 2002), pp. 476–483. https://cdn.aaai.org/AAAI/2002/AAAI02-072.pdf."}]}, {"type": "ref_text", "index": 8, "bbox": [0.116, 0.669, 0.882, 0.705], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "[4] Russell, S., and Norvig, P. Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach, fourth ed. Pearson, 2020."}]}, {"type": "ref_text", "index": 9, "bbox": [0.116, 0.718, 0.884, 0.755], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "[5] Stentz, A. The focussed D* algorithm for real-time replanning. In Proceedings of the International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence (IJCAI) (1995), pp. 1652–1659."}]}, {"type": "page_number", "index": 10, "bbox": [0.493, 0.938, 0.506, 0.952], "content": [{"type": "text", "content": "3"}]}]}], "is_full_document": true, "schema": "docvortex.middle", "schema_version": "2.0"}